diff --git a/spec/QUOTA.md b/spec/QUOTA.md
new file mode 100644
index 00000000..4124bae7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/spec/QUOTA.md
@@ -0,0 +1,63 @@
+# Codex quota contract (macOS phase one)
+
+This is a local, account-level quota observation, distinct from token usage, spend, and the remote collector's metrics. The Codex provider reads the locally available Codex session and queries the account's usage endpoint. Do not transmit credentials or quota data to the collector, include credentials in logs, or write credentials back. The endpoint and fields below are observed provider behavior, not a guarantee of a stable OpenAI API.
+
+## Snapshot
+
+A snapshot has the following logical fields. Optional values are nullable; a missing reading is **unknown**, not zero. Dates are instants (when serialized, ISO 8601 UTC); durations are seconds. IDs are stable machine identifiers, not localized presentation strings.
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `id` | Provider identifier, `codex` for this provider. |
+| `displayName` | Human-readable provider name. |
+| `fidelity` | One of `official`, `derived`, or `manual`: respectively a provider's own endpoint, an inferred reading, or a user-entered reading. Codex uses `official`, which does not imply an externally guaranteed API. |
+| `status` | One of `ok`, `stale`, `needsAuth`, `accessDenied`, `unsupported`, `error`, defined below. |
+| `windows` | Zero or more individually identified quota windows. |
+| `headlineId` | Nullable ID of the designated headline window; consumers resolve by ID, not array position. It must not select an unrelated extra just because the primary is absent. |
+| `block` | Nullable explanatory reason that usage is blocked, when known. Do not invent one from missing data. |
+| `account`, `plan` | Nullable account and subscription plan labels, respectively; they are not credentials. |
+| `observedAt` | Nullable time of the last successful observation, not the time an old observation was displayed. |
+| `source` | Description/identifier of the source of the observation. |
+| `nextAllowedAt` | Nullable earliest next permitted refresh time, if the provider is backing off. |
+| `waitingForRefresh` | True only when a persisted successful polling interval prevents a refresh and no observation is available in this process. Status remains `error` for compatibility; display this condition as waiting, not a failed request. |
+| `credits` | Nullable credit information: `hasCredits` and `unlimited` are nullable booleans, `balance` is a nullable decimal string. `limit` and `used` are nullable numeric absolute amounts in provider-reported spend units, with no inferred currency; `usedFraction` and `resetAt` describe the individual spend-control allowance. A balance of `"0"` is known zero, not unknown. The allowance's `usedFraction` is not clamped and may exceed 1. |
+
+A window contains `id`, `label`, `usedFraction`, `remaining`, `used`, `resetAt`, and `duration`. `id` is stable and unique within a snapshot; `label` is display text. For Codex windows, `usedFraction` and `used` are the reported percentage divided by 100, and `remaining` is 1 minus that fraction. These nullable fractions are not absolute counts and are not clamped: usage may exceed 1 and remaining may be negative. Zero is known zero, not unknown. `resetAt` is a nullable absolute instant and `duration` is a nullable window length in seconds. Unknown fields remain null, including an unknown fraction on an otherwise valid window.
+
+`primary_window` and `secondary_window` are source slots, not synonyms for five-hour and weekly windows: they can be swapped, have equal or unknown durations, or one can be missing. Identify a known-duration window by its source family and duration, adding a slot suffix for same-family duration collisions; use an `unknown:slot` fallback when duration is unknown. Namespace feature-scoped extras and code review separately from the main windows and one another. Never assume a particular duration from a primary or secondary slot.
+
+## Status and freshness
+
+* `ok`: a usable fresh observation was obtained.
+* `stale`: a previous successful observation is being displayed after a later refresh failed or became unavailable; preserve its original `observedAt` and do not present it as fresh.
+* `needsAuth`: no usable local session, or authentication is rejected.
+* `accessDenied`: the account is authenticated but access is explicitly denied; do not conflate absence of quota with a confirmed denial.
+* `unsupported`: the provider/account or response is known not to support this quota surface, rather than merely returning missing fields.
+* `error`: a network, decoding, service, or other failure that does not establish a more specific state.
+
+An authentication failure must not be represented as zero usage. If old data is retained, it must be visibly stale; a previous timestamp must never be advanced on an unsuccessful refresh. Rate limiting is a transient failure, not an empty successful observation. Respect server retry guidance and avoid repeatedly querying the endpoint while it is rate-limited.
+After restart, a successful poll's persisted interval can block a request even though the observation itself was not persisted. In that case `waitingForRefresh` is true until polling is allowed. Actual 429, decoding, network and service failures have `waitingForRefresh` false, including during a persisted failure backoff; they must remain visibly errors (or stale when an observation exists). Unknown legacy schedule provenance is not reported as a successful wait.
+
+## Desktop presentation
+
+Quota is presented in the existing Settings window as a provider card with progress, following the supplied `desktop-settings.png` design. Show the headline window selected by `headlineId`, its usage and reset when known, and the other available windows and account details without treating provider response order as window identity. Identify windows by their labels and durations; do not assume a primary slot is five-hour or a secondary slot is weekly. Display used and remaining together where space permits.
+
+An optional, separate edge-attached floating quota renderer may also be enabled. Its visual appearance must reproduce [Codenotch at commit `835550a342871779135b6484d6899161c5222f7c`](https://github.com/vinzdg/codenotch/tree/835550a342871779135b6484d6899161c5222f7c), while using this contract's actual Codex snapshot and status semantics. It is a presentation of the same local account data, not another provider, quota history, network source, or change to polling. Do not show fabricated providers or example readings as real data; currently this contract describes the Codex provider, not an implementation of every provider or the mobile document's full schema.
+
+The existing transient menu-bar popover or tray flyout may show a compact quota summary only when a known window is at least 90% used, a block is present, or status is `needsAuth`. Show at most two qualifying providers; open the existing Settings quota page for details.
+
+Claims of pixel-identical rendering require comparison of native screenshots against the pinned Codenotch reference at the same DPI, presentation state, and account data. No current native screenshot comparison proves pixel identity.
+
+Unknown usage and reset values are shown as `—` or unknown, never as zero or an invented reset. A known zero remains zero. Show non-official fidelity with `~`; status takes precedence over numerical readings. Retained readings must be visibly stale, with their original observation time or age, and must not appear fresh. Show authentication, access, unsupported, waiting, and error states distinctly rather than implying a successful reading. Display a known block separately from window usage, including its reason when available; do not infer a block from missing data or conflate it with a percentage. Progress graphics may be bounded visually, but labels must preserve reported over-limit usage and negative remaining values.
+
+## Codex usage endpoint
+
+`GET https://chatgpt.com/backend-api/wham/usage` uses the local Codex session's bearer access token and `ChatGPT-Account-Id`; `Accept: application/json` requests JSON. Never place either credential in fixtures or logs. `plan_type` is the plan when supplied. The main `rate_limit.primary_window` and `rate_limit.secondary_window` are independent; parse each without discarding a valid sibling because another is null or malformed. A window's numeric `used_percent` converts to `usedFraction` by dividing by 100 without clamping. `limit_window_seconds` gives duration. `reset_at` is Unix epoch seconds; when absent, `reset_after_seconds` is relative to the observation time. Null or omitted data stays unknown. A non-null 0 percentage is a valid measurement.
+`additional_rate_limits` is an array of feature-scoped rate-limit objects (`limit_name`, `metered_feature`, `rate_limit`), not a replacement for the main account windows. `code_review_rate_limit` may contain another pair. Preserve source scope when representing these windows. A malformed extra must not destroy usable main data. `spend_control.individual_limit` is a separate credit-backed allowance: its `limit` and `used` can be decimal strings, `used_percent` is a percentage, and `reset_at` is epoch seconds. The response's `credits` balance is distinct from that allowance and from unused reset credits at the separate `/wham/rate-limit-reset-credits` endpoint; none is interchangeable with another.
+
+Synthetic, credential-free examples: [`fixtures/codex-quota.json`](fixtures/codex-quota.json) shows main windows, an extra, a code-review window with unknown usage, and spend control; [`fixtures/codex-credit-quota.json`](fixtures/codex-credit-quota.json) shows missing rolling windows and a known zero balance. Their timestamps are examples, not current reset predictions. These are illustrative endpoint payloads, not captures of a real account.
+Both endpoint fixtures are raw provider responses, not snapshots; parsing either produces snapshot fidelity `official`.
+
+## Reference and attribution
+
+Endpoint and response research consulted [Codenotch](https://github.com/vinzdg/codenotch), particularly `Sources/Providers/CodexLocalProvider.swift` and `Sources/Providers/CodexUsage.swift`. Codenotch is MIT licensed, copyright (c) 2026 Vinz. This specification and its synthetic examples describe observed facts and do not reproduce Codenotch source code. If source code or a substantial portion is copied into the implementation, retain its full MIT copyright and permission notice with that code.
